{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, just wanted to let you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know we have some new merch at the merch \nstore celebrating 10 years of Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How did the universe begin? How can \nsomething come from nothing? One way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“solve” this most difficult of philosophical \nconundrums is to avoid it altogether. Maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18440,
    "dDurationMs": 11520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe didn’t begin at all. Maybe the \nBig Bang was just one in an endless cycle. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30520,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On February 11th, 2015 a new show called PBS Space \nTime appeared on YouTube. In the 10 years since,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together we've explored the insides of black holes \nand ventured across the edge of the universe and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44040,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen the beginning and end of time and the peeked \nat the underlying clockwork of nature. It's been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brain-breaking and existentially humbling journey. \nAnd we're so happy you have been on this ride with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58280,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us. Well, that ride continues. We have exciting \nnew plans for year 11 that will be revealed in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67640,
    "dDurationMs": 8920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due course. For now, two things you can do to help \nus get the anniversary celebration started. First,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76560,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "post in the comments to say how long you've been \nwatching ... and if you joined more recently how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82520,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much of the back catalog of over 400 videos did \nyou actually manage to get through. And second,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merch! We’ve got a limited edition 10 \nyear anniversary design as well as some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96040,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classic logo merch. Having this gear \ndoesn't just make you incredibly cool,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you also helps us keep going for another decade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No one wants the end of the universe. Maybe \nthat’s why the idea of a cyclic universe is so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112000,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appealing. So much so that it appears in mythical \ncosmologies of Hinduism, Buddhism, Zoroastrianism,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the cosmologies of the Norse, Mayans, \nEgyptians, Greeks, and no doubt more. It makes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense. We tend to extrapolate from the patterns \nwe see in nature. We see recurring cycles of day  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and night, of the seasons, of life and death. Why \nshouldn’t the entire universe go through cycles?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136040,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, the evidence has to support \nour extrapolations, and we now know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the evidence does not support cyclic \ncosmologies. Right? The universe and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself started at the Big Bang and space \nwill expand forever and that’s it. Or so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "says mainstream cosmology. But apparently \ncyclic cosmology has not lost its appeal,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155480,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because scientists have found a way \nto make it work for our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159160,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The original Big Bang model has all of space \noriginating in an infinitesimal point at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beginning of time, and expanding from there. \nThis fits a lot of observations of our universe. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The recession of the galaxies \nreveals the expansion of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the cosmic microwave background is \npretty clearly the afterglow of an early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot, dense state. But some observations \naren’t so easily explained. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181600,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our universe matter and energy are very evenly \nspread out, but in the basic Big Bang model there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn’t time for this smoothing to happen before \nthe expansion threw distant regions beyond causal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contact. This is the horizon problem. There’s \nalso the fact that a basic-Big-Bang universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn’t expected to be so perfectly flat, which \nrequires an uncannily perfect balance between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter and dark energy. And if the universe \nthat began in an extremely hot, dense state,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than certain relics of this phase should \npersist—so-called magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course we’ve talked about the horizon, \nflatness, and magnetic monopole problems,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we have the most popular solution—cosmic \ninflation. This proposes an extreme,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exponential expansion phase in the extremely \nearly universe. Inflation becomes the bang in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang, and it allows an initially smooth \nuniverse to be expanded beyond causal contact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235720,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as being nicely flattened \nspace and scattering those pesky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monopoles far enough apart that \nthey're unlikely to ever be seen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244720,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To top it all off, inflation explains how the \nuniverse got its large-scale structure. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicts quantum fluctuations in the inflaton \nfield, which became the gravitational seeds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that grew into galaxies and galaxy clusters. \nInflation goes further, predicting the those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations should lead to the same level of \nlumpiness at all size scales—so-called scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invariance. And that’s exactly what we see in \nthe lumps of the cosmic microwave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273160,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inflation does such a good job \nthat it’s practically mainstream,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276640,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there are some downsides. Modern versions \nof the idea predict that if inflation happened,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281960,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it never ended. Sure our little \npatch quit with that extreme growth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289040,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spawning our much-slower expanding universe. But \nas long as inflation didn’t stop everywhere all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294040,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at once, then out there, somewhere, this eternally \ninflating greater universe is blowing up forever,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly spawning bubble universes. \nSome find this idea a little extravagant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307520,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other issue with inflation is that \nit doesn’t avoid a beginning of time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor a point of infinite density—a singularity at \nthat beginning. You can push that singulary as far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back as you like, but in inflationary models \nit has to be there, just like in the regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang. And any theory that predicts \na singularity is automatically suspect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328200,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even if inflation didn’t have its issues, it’s \nworth exploring other options. So what about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "option where, instead of asking what happened at \nthe beginning of time, we ask what happens if time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never had a beginning? Cyclic cosmologies exist \nin many ancient traditions, but also in modern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmology. Soon after we noticed that the universe \nwas expanding, scientists came up with models in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which the universe eventually slows and starts \ncontracting, then bounces into a new Big Bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and repeats over and over. But these models \ndidn’t manage to do away with the beginning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time because both entropy and the lifespan \nof the universe had to increase with each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366920,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bounce. That means we couldn’t extrapolate the \nbouncing back in time indefinitely. There had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be a first. And anyway, these cyclic universes \ndidn’t solve the problems that inflation solves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379760,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that cyclic cosmologies can give \nus everything we want. To explain the horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385720,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flatness and monopole problems without \ninflation, and at the same time eliminate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that pesky beginning of the universe. In fact, \nit turns out that the same type of field that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396320,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes inflation can also be tweaked to give an \ninfinitely regenerating universe. This is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea of the ekpyrotic universe—named after the \ncyclic cosmology of the ancient Greek Stoics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410960,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the universe is rebirthed in \nfire—ekpyrosis—between unending cycles. By  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418120,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way, this is a very different idea to the \nconformal cyclic cosmology of Roger Penrose,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I refer you to our previous episode \nfor that equally awesome proposal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ekpyrotic universe was first proposed in 2001 \nin a paper by Justin Khoury and collaborators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, I’m going to come back to the ideas \nof this paper, but first I want to give the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of the story told in a followup paper \nby two of the original authors. In 2002,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paul Steinhardt and Neil Turok showed how the \nsame type of quantum field proposed to cause  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation—the inflaton field—can be tweaked to \nresurrect the universe rather than blowing it up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458320,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The extreme accelerating expansion of inflation \nis driven by the same type of quantum field as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463560,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we think now drives the relatively \nchill acceleration that we attribute  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to dark energy. It’s a scalar field—the \nsimplest type of quantum field in that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472480,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s just a simple numerical property—a field \nstrength—in space everywhere. The field also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a potential energy associated with that \nnumerical value. Sometimes the relationship  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between field value and its potential energy is \nsimple—stronger the field the more the energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sometimes it's complicated. For example, in some \nversions of inflation, the field value slowly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drops and energy decreases, but then the energy \nreaches a minimum value and any further change  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the field would add more energy. Therefore \nthe field becomes stable and inflation stops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506440,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are lots of different ways you can \nrelate the field energy to the field value. And,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511440,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as shown by Steinhardt and Turok one of \nthose ways gives you a cyclic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We normally think of dark energy as being due \nto a constant energy density everywhere in space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523800,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that does not change over time. But maybe dark \nenergy changes only very slowly. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if there’s this quantum field that is slowly \nchanging in value—say, decreasing—with barely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538000,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "noticeable changes in the associated energy. Then \nwe have accelerating expansion far into the future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544440,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even as the field value drops. But eventually the \nenergy in that field fades and becomes negative,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551680,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and as that happens acceleration slow, then \nexpansion slows and the universe briefly halts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560640,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then the universe recollapses. The field \npotential bottoms out in a minimum and rises  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to zero. Now, we might expect the \nfield to get stuck in that minimum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573200,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "however during this contraction, \ngravitational potential energy is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converted into kinetic energy of the field \nso that the field value blows past this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582680,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimum. In the final phase of contraction \nthe field has no potential energy—no “dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589680,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy” equivalent—and the kinetic energy \nof the field gets converted into radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596600,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This liberates the universe from \nthe constraints of this quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599440,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field and it quickly starts expanding again. \nThe radiation spawns matter and then dissipates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607360,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the matter dominates for a while, \nand finally dark energy takes over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we find ourselves back where we \nstarted in the roughly the modern era."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does this version of a cyclic universe \nsolve all our problems? Well, the magic happens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the contraction phase—what the authors call \nthe ekpyrotic period. When the universe was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at its largest, matter was so far-flung that the \nonly meaningful energy in the universe was in its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635680,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scalar field, and the only meaningful structure in \nthe universe were the quantum fluctuations in that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641880,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. Now as the universe collapses very slowly, \nthose fluctuations are amplified. The shape of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scalar field is tuned so that these fluctuations \nhave a scale-invariance—equal frequency for all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656160,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sizes of lumps, just as is observed in \nthe CMB and as is predicted by inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663880,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That contraction phase also smooths out the \nuniverse, solving the horizon problem. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe does not reach arbitrarily high \ntemperatures, so no magnetic monopoles ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to be created. There’s no singularity, and \nthere’s no significant difference between one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681520,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycle and the next, so this model is consistent \nwith cycles extending back in time forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687240,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steinhardt and Turok claim that the \nscalar field needed to achieve all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this magic is no more finely tuned than \nthe field needed to achieve inflation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696720,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so this cyclic model is just as plausible as \nthe inflationary model because they both result in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702360,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same observables—at least as far as current \nobservational sensitivity allows. There are .."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is there any more motivation to believe that a \nfield of the needed variety actually exists? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these guys say yes, and the mechanism \nwas proposed the year prior by a team  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720400,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including these authors and led by Justin Khoury."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This does require a little more than a \nmodification of the inflaton field. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727640,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires an entire new dimension of space. 6 new \ndimensions really. One motivation for the type of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scalar field needed, with its particular potential \nenergy curve, lies within M-theory. This is an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encompassing framework for string theory, in which \nour universe, with its 3 dimensions of space and 1  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time, exists as a single slice in a greater \nobject with 4 large spatial dimensions. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 6 coiled, compact dimensions, but we don’t \nneed to worry about those for this description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe would be something called \na brane—short for membrane—living within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the higher dimensional space—itself \ncalled the bulk. In this picture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe is one of the boundary layers of the \nbulk. We call it the visible brane. Normally this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brane is just chillin—it’s pretty empty and is \nnot changing in size. Things get interesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782160,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when another free-floating brane within the \nbulk—called hidden brane—smashes into us. Which,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apparently, is something that can happen. \nTry not to let it keep you up at night. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794200,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision dumps a bunch of energy into \nthe visible brane, sparking a big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800880,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This connects to the description \nof the scalar field because we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpret the value of that field as \nthe distance between the visible brane  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this incoming hidden brane. So on \nthe graph we saw earlier, movement to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816440,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the left—decreasing field value—corresponds \nto decreasing distance between the branes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821760,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Khoury paper assumes a simpler form of the \npotential than Steinhardt and Turok, with energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decreasing exponentially as the branes approach, \nwhich is like a purely attractive force between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the branes. In the more complex version where \npotential energy decreases then increases again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have attraction then repulsion of the \nbranes. Either way, when the branes collide,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy is dumped into the visible brane \ncausing space there to start expanding. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hidden brane recoils, propelled back \nthe way it came with the energy of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bounce until eventually it’s pulled inwards again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this interpretation, the quantum fluctuations \nof matter manifest as wiggles in the incoming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867560,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hidden brane. These result in different parts \nof that brane arriving at different times,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873920,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so there are variations in the start time of \nexpansion in the visible brane. These ultimately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881600,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result in density and temperature fluctuations \nin the resulting cosmic microwave background,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which, again, have scale invariance. And we \ncan interpret the solutions to the horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flatness, and magnetic monopole problems in the \ncontext of colliding branes. Both the visible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899160,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and hidden branes exist long before the \ncollision, and so they can reach thermal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equilibrium over a large enough region \nto explain the smoothness of the CMB. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909440,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "branes can be very flat over the range that \neventually become the observable universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915480,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so there’s flatness achieved. And this type of \nBig Bang doesn’t start as a singularity—there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a limit to how hot it gets—and so we don’t \nneed to create magnetic monopoles here either."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, did we just manage to save \nthe universe from ever ending,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or save it from ever starting for \nthat matter? And at the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936320,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did we save ourselves from having to be part \nof an eternally inflating multiverse? Let’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not get ahead of ourselves. For one thing, \nif this ekypyrotic behavior is due to our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe colliding with others within a higher \ndimensional space, that’s hardly less extravagant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952960,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the eternal inflation. This M-theory stuff \nmay not be the cause of the peculiar potential,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961080,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but even so, we’re going to want ways to test \nthis against the also-untested inflationary model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the ekpyrotic model predicts almost \nexactly the same observables as inflation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974320,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are potential differences. There may be \nslight differences in the spectrum of density  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978440,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations, but more concretely we would \nexpect differences in the gravitational waves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced in the inflationary versus \nekpyrotic Big Bangs. In either case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the extremely energetic early universe would \nhave generated gigantic gravitational waves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994320,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weighted towards lower frequency in the ekpyrotic \ncase compared to inflation. No currently planned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detector will be able to see these waves, but it’s \nconceivable that one day we’ll build a detector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006360,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can sense they now extremely faint buzz of \nancient spacetime ripples, and read from the m  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013600,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nature of the beginning of this universe. \nThose waves may also have left a signature on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019080,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the matter that formed soon after, and we may one \nday be readable in the polarisation of the CMB."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027680,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, it’s crazy to even imagine that we \nmay one day be able to test ideas like this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034840,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and actually have a good idea, one way or another,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether there’s an infinite multiverse \nextending through inflating space, or if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043520,
    "dDurationMs": 35320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re just one universe in an endless temporal \nchain of expanding and contracting spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
